NAME
    ls, ll, la, dir — List the contents of a directory, with varying
    degrees of nosiness.

SYNOPSIS
    ls [OPTIONS] [path]
    ll [path]
    la [path]
    dir [path]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'ls' command looks inside a directory and tells you what it found.

    By default, it politely ignores files whose names begin with '.',
    because some things are better left unseen.

    Directories are displayed with a trailing '/', so you can tell your
    folders from your files without having to emotionally inspect them.

    If the requested path does not exist, 'ls' reports the tragedy and
    leaves you to deal with it.

ALIASES
    ll
        Equivalent to 'ls -l'. Because typing two extra characters is
        apparently too much work.

    la
        Equivalent to 'ls -a'. For when you're ready to confront the
        hidden files you've been avoiding.

    dir
        Equivalent to 'ls'. For users who like their command names
        slightly more verbose and Windows-flavored.

OPTIONS
    -a
        Show all entries, including hidden files.

        Use this when you suspect there is a .secret-file lurking
        somewhere and you simply must know.

    -l
        Use long format.

        Displays permissions, human-readable size, modification time,
        and the name. In other words: 'ls', but now judging your files
        by their metadata.

ARGUMENTS
    path
        Directory to list. Defaults to '.' if omitted.

        Because even when you provide absolutely nothing, we still
        know where you probably meant.

OUTPUT
    Normal mode:
        Files are listed alphabetically, case-insensitively.
        Directories receive a trailing '/'.

    Long mode:
        Each entry includes:

            permissions  size  modification-time  name

        Directories display '-' instead of a file size, presumably
        because measuring a directory's size would invite questions
        nobody wants to answer.

EXAMPLES
    > ls
        Lists the current directory.

        Congratulations. You have successfully looked around.

    > ls src
        Lists the contents of 'src'.

        Ah yes, the place where the important stuff supposedly lives.

    > ls -a
        Shows hidden files too.

        .git has entered the chat.

    > ls -l
        Shows detailed information about every visible entry.

        For when "there are some files here" is simply not enough
        information.

    > ll
        The lazy person's long listing.

        Same information, fewer keystrokes. Humanity advances.

    > la
        Shows everything, including hidden entries.

        You weren't supposed to see that.

    > dir
        Lists the current directory.

        Same as 'ls', but wearing a slightly different hat.

    > ls definitely-not-a-real-directory
        No such path: ...

        The filesystem has declined your invitation.

FUN FACTS
    - Entries are sorted alphabetically without caring about case.
    - Hidden files are excluded by default, because plausible deniability
      is an important filesystem feature.
    - Directories get '/', which is basically a tiny sign saying
      "there's more nonsense in here."
    - 'll' is not a real separate command. It is 'ls' wearing '-l'.
    - 'la' is similarly 'ls' wearing '-a'.
    - 'dir' exists so nobody can accuse this CLI of lacking aliases.

